Derived rather than copied verbatim. Each item was:

  * normalised  - option prefixes ("A)21") stripped, the correct letter mapped
                  to an index, whitespace collapsed, missing spaces after
                  sentence punctuation repaired
  * filtered    - five distinct options required, stems over 180 characters
                  dropped, anything referring to a figure or table dropped
  * classified  - the dataset carries no topic labels, so bucketing to our
                  topics is our own work, by keyword with an exclusion veto
  * VERIFIED    - every promoted item is checked arithmetically by
                  scripts/verify_aqua.py. The dataset's answers are
                  crowd-written and some are wrong: "15 times a number gives
                  150" was labelled 15, when 15 x 15 = 225 and the correct
                  value was not among the options at all. An item whose
                  template the verifier does not recognise is rejected rather
                  than trusted.
  * annotated   - `guided` items carry a hand-written hint sentence naming the
                  first move. That sentence is ours, not the dataset's.

Rationales are used as shipped. They are NOT used to build worked examples:
those are hand-written, because a broken worked example is the most visible
possible failure in a product that claims to teach.

Provenance for every item is recorded in `source_ref` as "<split>:<line>".
